import DocsLayout from "../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";

const bindingCode = `let adapter = McpRemoteTool::connect(
    local_descriptor,
    "lookup_issue",
    ProviderEndpoint::https("https://mcp.example.com/v1/")?,
    McpServerIdentity::new("issue-service", "2026.09.0")?,
    Arc::new(schema_registry),
    Arc::new(authorization_provider),
    McpHttpOptions::default(),
)
.await?;`;

const reconciliationCode = `let reconciliation = ToolReconciliationHandoff::result(
    live_fence,
    unknown_invocation,
    reconciliation_event_id,
    authoritative_result,
)?;

match executor.commit_tool_reconciliation(reconciliation).await? {
    ToolReconciliationOutcome::Committed { .. } => acknowledge(),
    ToolReconciliationOutcome::Idempotent { .. } => acknowledge(),
}`;
---

<DocsLayout
  title="Bind one MCP Tool without weakening durability."
  description="Discover a stateless MCP 2026-07-28 Tool once, pin its identity and schemas locally, and preserve ambiguous write outcomes."
  section="MCP Remote Tool"
>
  <aside
    class="docs-callout docs-callout--boundary"
    aria-labelledby="mcp-boundary-title"
  >
    <h2 id="mcp-boundary-title">Implemented strict client profile</h2>
    <p>
      <code>McpRemoteTool</code> implements one client-side remote Tool binding: protocol
      <code>2026-07-28</code>, modern discovery, stateless Streamable HTTP, and
      complete JSON responses. It is not an MCP server and it is not a claim of
      complete MCP conformance.
    </p>
  </aside>

  <section aria-labelledby="mcp-profile-title">
    <h2 id="mcp-profile-title">Accept one deliberately narrow profile</h2>
    <dl class="docs-definition-list">
      <div>
        <dt>Transport</dt>
        <dd>
          Verified HTTPS in production; literal-loopback HTTP only for managed
          sidecars and tests. Redirects, HTTP retries, sessions, and SSE fail
          closed.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Discovery</dt>
        <dd>
          One modern discovery at startup, bounded Tool catalog paging, exact
          protocol version, required Tools capability, and pinned self-reported
          server name/version.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Schemas</dt>
        <dd>
          Remote input/output JSON Schemas must exactly match RFC 8785 canonical
          bytes in the authoritative local registry. Remote annotations never
          define local risk or policy.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Results</dt>
        <dd>
          Complete structured content only, bounded and validated locally. The
          current profile rejects artifact, image, audio, embedded-resource, and
          resource-link materialization.
        </dd>
      </div>
    </dl>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="mcp-binding-title">
    <h2 id="mcp-binding-title">Freeze the binding at readiness</h2>
    <p>
      Build the local <code>ToolDescriptor</code> from reviewed configuration. A server
      upgrade or schema change requires a new binding; runtime calls never rediscover
      silently.
    </p>
    <div class="docs-code" data-code-block>
      <div class="docs-code-head">
        <span>Rust · startup discovery</span>
        <button
          class="copy-button affordance"
          type="button"
          data-copy-button
          data-copy-target="mcp-binding-code"
        >
          <span data-copy-label aria-live="polite">Copy</span>
        </button>
      </div>
      <pre id="mcp-binding-code" tabindex="0"><code>{bindingCode}</code></pre>
    </div>
    <p>
      TLS authenticates the configured endpoint. The discovered implementation
      name/version is self-reported metadata: pinning detects drift, but does
      not replace mTLS or an authenticated gateway when stronger server identity
      is required.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="mcp-secrets-title">
    <h2 id="mcp-secrets-title">Resolve secrets per admitted attempt</h2>
    <p>
      Multi-tenant deployments should implement <code
        >McpAuthorizationProvider</code
      >
      over vault-backed handles. The adapter waits for its call gate before resolving
      attempt credentials, redacts authorization from <code>Debug</code>, clears
      the slot after exchange, and never copies credentials into MCP metadata.
      Static bearer authorization is intended only for controlled single-tenant
      bindings.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="mcp-failure-title">
    <h2 id="mcp-failure-title">Keep a lost write ambiguous</h2>
    <ol class="evidence-list">
      <li>
        <h3>Validate locally</h3>
        <p>Reject input outside the pinned schema before network dispatch.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h3>Call once</h3>
        <p>Each admitted durable attempt sends one <code>tools/call</code>.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h3>Do not invent success</h3>
        <p>
          If a locally declared write loses its response, return <code
            >Unknown</code
          > external effect, <code>AmbiguousExternalOutcome</code>, and <code
            >ReconcileFirst</code
          >.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h3>Validate the result</h3>
        <p>
          Accept only complete structured output that passes the pinned local
          output schema.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="mcp-reconciliation-title">
    <h2 id="mcp-reconciliation-title">
      Reconcile without calling the Tool again
    </h2>
    <p>
      A trusted worker or operations boundary may convert an exact
      <code>Unknown</code> physical attempt into a validated result or error. The
      runtime binds the evidence to the invocation and attempt, validates result JSON
      against the frozen local schema, and commits a distinct audit event in one fenced
      PostgreSQL transaction. It never resolves or dispatches the Tool provider.
    </p>
    <div class="docs-code" data-code-block>
      <div class="docs-code-head">
        <span>Rust · authoritative reconciliation</span>
        <button
          class="copy-button affordance"
          type="button"
          data-copy-button
          data-copy-target="mcp-reconciliation-code"
        >
          <span data-copy-label aria-live="polite">Copy</span>
        </button>
      </div>
      <pre
        id="mcp-reconciliation-code"
        tabindex="0"><code>{reconciliationCode}</code></pre>
    </div>
    <p>
      Exact retries are idempotent. A handoff retains its authoritative evidence
      across persistence failures and can be rebound only to a new live fence
      for the same run.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="mcp-exclusions-title">
    <h2 id="mcp-exclusions-title">Know what this profile rejects</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Stateful sessions, SSE, legacy initialization, and reconnect.</li>
      <li>MRTR, Tasks, incomplete results, progress, and artifact content.</li>
      <li>
        Tools requiring StateKnot idempotency-key injection; generic MCP has no
        safe standard carrier for that durable key.
      </li>
      <li>
        MCP server, resources, prompts, roots, sampling, elicitation, logging,
        and MCP Apps support.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="mcp-proof-title">
    <h2 id="mcp-proof-title">Run the profile and durable contracts</h2>
    <div class="docs-code" data-code-block>
      <div class="docs-code-head">
        <span>Shell · MCP contract and real PostgreSQL recovery</span>
        <button
          class="copy-button affordance"
          type="button"
          data-copy-button
          data-copy-target="mcp-proof-command"
        >
          <span data-copy-label aria-live="polite">Copy</span>
        </button>
      </div>
      <pre
        id="mcp-proof-command"
        tabindex="0"><code>cargo test -p stateknot-integrations --test mcp_contract --locked
cargo test -p stateknot-integrations --test mcp_durable --locked -- --test-threads=1</code></pre>
    </div>
    <p>
      The suite proves discovery order, identity/schema pinning, credential
      headers, annotation distrust, pre-registration drift rejection, and
      ambiguous lost writes. The PostgreSQL 16/17 matrix additionally proves
      durable-before-dispatch state, a lost write response, <code>Unknown</code
      >, duplicate suppression, authoritative reconciliation, and one physical
      network call. Read the full <a
        href="https://github.com/StateKnot/StateKnot/blob/main/docs/mcp-remote-tool.md"
        >profile and operations contract</a
      > and the <a href="/docs/mcp-conformance/"
        >frozen MCP conformance status</a
      >. StateKnot does not claim complete official MCP client or server
      conformance.
    </p>
  </section>
</DocsLayout>
